IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), Part V, general provisions, ss 94-101 (s 102 is shown blank in this text).
-- LAYER 1: the law, objectively. Plain-text rendering by a converter script, not the official text.
-- BAR POLARITY: a "shall not be payable" function answers "is the person barred?": JUST TRUE = the text says not payable; JUST FALSE = the text says no bar (a failed "while", an exception); NOTHING = silent.
-- DEEMING POLARITY (ss 98-101): the function answers "does THIS SECTION treat the accident as arising out of and in the course of the employment?".
-- A failed "if" limb is JUST FALSE for the section only. Whether the accident arises in fact, apart from the section, is a judgment the text does not supply (fork F6).

§ `Part V -- Benefit for industrial injuries: general provisions`

§§ `s 94 -- Right to industrial injuries benefit`

-- s 94(1): "Industrial injuries benefit shall be payable where an employed earner suffers personal injury caused ... by accident arising out of and in the course of his employment, being employed earner's employment."
-- This is the kind of injury other parts ask about (seam S19, the name is the plan's). "Where" is not "only if": an injury not of the kind is not said to be outside the benefit, so failure is NOTHING (fork F7).
-- src:2280 | (1) Industrial injuries benefit shall be payable where an employed earner suffers personal injury caused ... by accident arising out of and in the course of his employment, being employed earner’s employment.
@ref s 94(1) (src:2280)
GIVEN `s 2(1)(a) the person is an employed earner` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person suffers personal injury caused by an accident` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident arises out of and in the course of the person's employment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employment is employed earner's employment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 94(1) the personal injury is of a kind mentioned in section 94(1)` MEANS
    IF   (`s 2(1)(a) the person is an employed earner` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
         AND `the person suffers personal injury caused by an accident`
         AND (`the accident arises out of and in the course of the person's employment` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
         AND (`the employment is employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "shall be payable where" names a case in which the benefit is payable and does not say that it is payable only there

-- src:2280 | (1) Industrial injuries benefit shall be payable where an employed earner suffers personal injury caused ... by accident arising out of and in the course of his employment, being employed earner’s employment.
@ref s 94(1) (src:2280)
GIVEN `the personal injury is of a kind mentioned in section 94(1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 94(1) industrial injuries benefit is payable` MEANS
    IF   `the personal injury is of a kind mentioned in section 94(1)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "shall be payable where" is not "only if"

-- s 94(2): "Industrial injuries benefit consists of the following benefits": (a) disablement benefit, (b) reduced earnings allowance, (c) retirement allowance, (d) industrial death benefit. "Consists of" closes the list.
-- src:2281 | (2) Industrial injuries benefit consists of the following benefits—
-- src:2282 | (a) disablement benefit payable in accordance with sections 103 to 105 below, paragraphs 2 and 3 of Schedule 7 below and Parts II and III of that Schedule;
-- src:2283 | (b) reduced earnings allowance payable in accordance with Part IV;
-- src:2284 | (c) retirement allowance payable in accordance with Part V; and
-- src:2285 | (d) industrial death benefit, payable in accordance with Part VI.
@ref s 94(2) (src:2281-2285)
GIVEN benefit IS A Benefit
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 94(2) the benefit is part of industrial injuries benefit` MEANS
    CONSIDER benefit
    WHEN `disablement benefit` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `reduced earnings allowance` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `retirement allowance` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `industrial death benefit` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Industrial injuries benefit consists of the following benefits"

-- s 94(3): "an accident arising in the course of an employed earner's employment shall be taken, in the absence of evidence to the contrary, also to have arisen out of that employment."
-- DEEMING POLARITY (fork F6): the function answers whether THIS SUBSECTION takes the accident to have arisen out of the employment.
-- "In the absence of evidence to the contrary" is an unless-type condition, so with evidence to the contrary the answer is JUST FALSE for the subsection only (the presumption does not run).
-- The subsection does not say the accident did not in fact arise out of the employment, and the function does not ask that.
-- An accident that did not arise in the course of an employed earner's employment is outside the sentence's subject, so the answer there is NOTHING.
-- src:2286 | (3) For the purposes of industrial injuries benefit an accident arising in the course of an employed earner’s employment shall be taken, in the absence of evidence to the contrary, also to have arisen out of that employment.
@ref s 94(3) (src:2286)
GIVEN `the accident arises in the course of an employed earner's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `there is evidence to the contrary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 94(3) the accident is taken to have arisen out of the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the accident arises in the course of an employed earner's employment`) THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the presumption is for "an accident arising in the course of an employed earner's employment"; for any other accident the subsection is silent
        IF `there is evidence to the contrary` THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall be taken, in the absence of evidence to the contrary, also to have arisen out of that employment" (the presumption does not run; this subsection only)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 94(4): regulations may fix the day treated as the day of the accident for night workers (a regulation-making power; inert, machinery).

-- s 94(5): "Subject to sections 117, 119 and 120 below, industrial injuries benefit shall not be payable in respect of an accident happening while the earner is outside Great Britain."
-- The exceptions are in other parts (seam S20, three MAYBE BOOLEAN inputs named as the plan names them). An exception that applies means no bar.
-- A failed "while" is the text's no bar (fork F8, a marginal reading).
-- src:2288 | (5) Subject to sections 117, 119 and 120 below, industrial injuries benefit shall not be payable in respect of an accident happening while the earner is outside Great Britain.
@ref s 94(5) (src:2288)
GIVEN `the accident happens while the earner is outside Great Britain` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 117 the accident falls within the exception` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 119 the accident falls within the exception` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `s 120 the accident falls within the exception` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 94(5) the person is barred from industrial injuries benefit for an accident outside Great Britain` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the accident happens while the earner is outside Great Britain`) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the bar is for "an accident happening while the earner is outside Great Britain"
        IF (`s 117 the accident falls within the exception` EQUALS JUST TRUE) OR (`s 119 the accident falls within the exception` EQUALS JUST TRUE) OR (`s 120 the accident falls within the exception` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subject to sections 117, 119 and 120 below"
        IF (`s 117 the accident falls within the exception` EQUALS JUST FALSE) AND (`s 119 the accident falls within the exception` EQUALS JUST FALSE) AND (`s 120 the accident falls within the exception` EQUALS JUST FALSE) THEN JUST TRUE    -- the text says no (a flat negative): "industrial injuries benefit shall not be payable in respect of an accident happening while the earner is outside Great Britain"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether an exception in section 117, 119 or 120 applies is those sections' answer

-- s 94(6): "In the following provisions of this Part of this Act “work” in the contexts “incapable of work” and “incapacity for work” means work which the person in question can be reasonably expected to do."
-- This is the incapacity test that Schedule 7 asks about (seam S36, the name is the plan's). The person is incapable of work in this sense only if incapable of work he can reasonably be expected to do.
-- src:2289 | (6) In the following provisions of this Part of this Act “work” in the contexts “incapable of work” and “incapacity for work” means work which the person in question can be reasonably expected to do.
@ref s 94(6) (src:2289)
GIVEN `the word "work" is used in the context "incapable of work" or "incapacity for work" in a provision of this Part following section 94(6)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is incapable of the work in question` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the work in question is work which the person can be reasonably expected to do` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 94(6) the person is incapable of work, "work" meaning work the person can reasonably be expected to do` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the word "work" is used in the context "incapable of work" or "incapacity for work" in a provision of this Part following section 94(6)`) THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the definition is for "the following provisions of this Part of this Act"
        IF `the person is incapable of the work in question` AND `the work in question is work which the person can be reasonably expected to do` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“work” ... means work which the person in question can be reasonably expected to do"

§§ `s 95 -- Relevant employments`

-- s 95(1): in s 94, s 95 and ss 98 to 109 "employed earner's employment" "shall be taken to include" any employment by virtue of which a person is, or is treated by regulations as being for the purposes of industrial injuries benefit, an employed earner.
-- "Include": an employment that is neither is not said to be outside the term.
-- src:2291 | (1) In section 94 above, this section and sections 98 to 109 below “employed earner’s employment” shall be taken to include any employment by virtue of which a person is, or is treated by regulations as being for the purposes of industrial injuries benefit, an employed earner.
@ref s 95(1) (src:2291)
GIVEN `s 2(1)(a) by virtue of the employment the person is an employed earner` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `by virtue of the employment the person is treated by regulations as being, for the purposes of industrial injuries benefit, an employed earner` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95(1) the employment is employed earner's employment` MEANS
    IF   (`s 2(1)(a) by virtue of the employment the person is an employed earner` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
         OR (`by virtue of the employment the person is treated by regulations as being, for the purposes of industrial injuries benefit, an employed earner` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "shall be taken to include" any such employment; an employment that is neither is not stated to be outside the term

-- s 95(2): regulations may provide that a prescribed employment is not treated as employed earner's employment (a regulation-making power; inert, machinery). The treatment it produces enters s 95(3) as an input.

-- s 95(3): "an employment shall be an employed earner's employment in relation to an accident if (and only if) it is, or is treated by regulations as being, such an employment when the accident occurs."
-- src:2293 | (3) For the purposes of the provisions of this Act mentioned in subsection (1) above an employment shall be an employed earner’s employment in relation to an accident if (and only if) it is, or is treated by regulations as being, such an employment when the accident occurs.
@ref s 95(3) (src:2293)
GIVEN `the employment is, when the accident occurs, an employed earner's employment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employment is, when the accident occurs, treated by regulations as being an employed earner's employment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95(3) the employment is employed earner's employment in relation to the accident` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`the employment is, when the accident occurs, an employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST TRUE) OR (`the employment is, when the accident occurs, treated by regulations as being an employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN JUST TRUE
        IF (`the employment is, when the accident occurs, an employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST FALSE) AND (`the employment is, when the accident occurs, treated by regulations as being an employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST FALSE) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if (and only if) it is, or is treated by regulations as being, such an employment when the accident occurs"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): one of the two limbs is not supplied

-- s 95(4): before 6th April 1975 a reference to "employed earner" or "employed earner's employment" in the industrial injuries and diseases provisions is a reference to an "insured person" or "insurable employment" within the meaning of the older provisions.
-- The older provisions are not in the text; the function says only when the construction applies.
-- src:2294 | (4) Any reference in the industrial injuries and diseases provisions to an “employed earner” or “employed earner’s employment” is to be construed, in relation to any time before 6th April 1975, as a reference respectively to an “insured person” or “insurable employment” within the meaning of the provisions relating to industrial injuries and diseases which were in force at that time.
@ref s 95(4) (src:2294)
GIVEN `the time to which the reference relates` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95(4) the reference is construed as a reference to an insured person or insurable employment` MEANS
    IF   `the time to which the reference relates` LESS THAN Date 6 4 1975
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the construction is "in relation to any time before 6th April 1975"; for later times the subsection is silent

-- s 95(5): "the industrial injuries and diseases provisions" means (a) s 95 and ss 96 to 110; (b) any other provisions of this Act so far as they relate to those sections; and (c) provisions of the Administration Act, Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998 or Part II of the Social Security Contributions (Transfer of Functions, etc.) Act 1999, so far as they so relate.
-- Section 95A falls between 95 and 96 and is not in "this section and sections 96 to 110"; it comes in, if at all, under (b), by relating to those sections.
-- src:2295 | (5) In subsection (4) above “the industrial injuries and diseases provisions” means—
-- src:2296 | (a) this section and sections 96 to 110 below;
-- src:2297 | (b) any other provisions of this Act so far as they relate to those sections; and
-- src:2298 | (c) any provisions of the Administration Act, Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998 or Part II of the Social Security Contributions (Transfer of Functions, etc.) Act 1999, so far as they so relate.
@ref s 95(5) (src:2295-2298)
GIVEN `the provision is section 95 or one of sections 96 to 110` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provision is another provision of this Act so far as it relates to those sections` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provision is a provision of the Administration Act, Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998 or Part II of the Social Security Contributions (Transfer of Functions, etc.) Act 1999 so far as it so relates` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95(5) the provision is among the industrial injuries and diseases provisions` MEANS
    IF   `the provision is section 95 or one of sections 96 to 110`
         OR `the provision is another provision of this Act so far as it relates to those sections`
         OR `the provision is a provision of the Administration Act, Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998 or Part II of the Social Security Contributions (Transfer of Functions, etc.) Act 1999 so far as it so relates`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“the industrial injuries and diseases provisions” means"

§§ `s 95A -- Employment training schemes etc`

-- s 95A(1): in the industrial injuries and diseases provisions a reference to employed earner's employment "shall be taken to include participation in an employment training scheme or employment training course of a prescribed description".
-- src:2300 | (1) In the industrial injuries and diseases provisions any reference to employed earner's employment shall be taken to include participation in an employment training scheme or employment training course of a prescribed description (and “employed earner” shall be construed accordingly).
@ref s 95A(1) (src:2300)
GIVEN `the person participates in an employment training scheme or employment training course` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the scheme or course is of a prescribed description` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95A(1) the participation is employed earner's employment` MEANS
    IF   `the person participates in an employment training scheme or employment training course` AND (`the scheme or course is of a prescribed description` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "shall be taken to include" participation of a prescribed description; other participation is not stated to be outside the term, and the descriptions are prescribed outside the text

-- s 95A(2): "a reference to an employer, in relation to any such participation, shall be taken to be a prescribed person."
-- src:2301 | (2) In those provisions, a reference to an employer, in relation to any such participation, shall be taken to be a prescribed person.
@ref s 95A(2) (src:2301)
GIVEN `the participation is of the kind described in subsection (1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person is a prescribed person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95A(2) the person is the employer in relation to the participation` MEANS
    IF   (`the participation is of the kind described in subsection (1)` EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND (`the person is a prescribed person` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): who is a prescribed person is for regulations that are not among the sources, and the subsection does not say that no one else is the employer

-- s 95A(3): "industrial injuries and diseases provisions" has the same meaning as in section 95(4) above. (The term is defined in s 95(5); fork F10.)
-- src:2302 | (3) In this section “industrial injuries and diseases provisions” has the same meaning as in section 95(4) above.
@ref s 95A(3) (src:2302)
GIVEN `the provision is section 95 or one of sections 96 to 110` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provision is another provision of this Act so far as it relates to those sections` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provision is a provision of the Administration Act, Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998 or Part II of the Social Security Contributions (Transfer of Functions, etc.) Act 1999 so far as it so relates` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 95A(3) the provision is among the industrial injuries and diseases provisions` MEANS
    `s 95(5) the provision is among the industrial injuries and diseases provisions` `the provision is section 95 or one of sections 96 to 110` `the provision is another provision of this Act so far as it relates to those sections` `the provision is a provision of the Administration Act, Chapter II of Part I of the Social Security Act 1998 or Part II of the Social Security Contributions (Transfer of Functions, etc.) Act 1999 so far as it so relates`

§§ `s 96 -- Persons treated as employers for certain purposes`

-- s 96: regulations may provide for a prescribed person to be treated as the earner's employer in the cases (a) and (b) (a regulation-making power; inert, machinery).

§§ `s 97 -- Accidents in course of illegal employments`

-- s 97(3): "relevant employment" means (a) in relation to an accident, the employment out of and in the course of which the accident arises; and (b) in relation to a prescribed disease or injury, the employment to the nature of which the disease or injury is due.
-- src:2317 | (3) In subsection (2) above “relevant employment” means—
-- src:2318 | (a) in relation to an accident, the employment out of and in the course of which the accident arises; and
-- src:2319 | (b) in relation to a prescribed disease or injury, the employment to the nature of which the disease or injury is due.
@ref s 97(3) (src:2317-2319)
DECLARE `s 97(3) Subject of the claim` IS ONE OF
    `s 97(3) an accident`
    `s 97(3) a prescribed disease or injury`

-- src:2317 | (3) In subsection (2) above “relevant employment” means—
-- src:2318 | (a) in relation to an accident, the employment out of and in the course of which the accident arises; and
-- src:2319 | (b) in relation to a prescribed disease or injury, the employment to the nature of which the disease or injury is due.
@ref s 97(3) (src:2317-2319)
GIVEN subject IS A `s 97(3) Subject of the claim`
      `the employment is the employment out of and in the course of which the accident arises` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employment is the employment to the nature of which the disease or injury is due` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 97(3) the employment is the relevant employment` MEANS
    CONSIDER subject
    WHEN `s 97(3) an accident` THEN IF `the employment is the employment out of and in the course of which the accident arises` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“relevant employment” means (a) in relation to an accident, the employment out of and in the course of which the accident arises"
    WHEN `s 97(3) a prescribed disease or injury` THEN IF `the employment is the employment to the nature of which the disease or injury is due` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) in relation to a prescribed disease or injury, the employment to the nature of which the disease or injury is due"

-- s 97(1) and (2): subsection (2) has effect where a claim is made for industrial injuries benefit in respect of an accident, or of a prescribed disease or injury, or an application is made under section 29 of the Social Security Act 1998 for a declaration.
-- Then "The Secretary of State may direct that the relevant employment shall ... be treated as having been employed earner's employment notwithstanding that" the contract was void or the employed person was not lawfully employed (fork F9).
-- Whether the Secretary of State has so directed is the official's judgment: a plain input.
-- src:2311 | (1) Subsection (2) below has effect in any case where—
-- src:2312 | (a) a claim is made for industrial injuries benefit in respect of an accident, or of a prescribed disease or injury; or
-- src:2313 | (b) an application is made under section 29 of the Social Security Act 1998 for a declaration that an accident was an industrial accident, or for a corresponding declaration as to a prescribed disease or injury.
-- src:2314 | (2) The Secretary of State may direct that the relevant employment shall, in relation to that accident, disease or injury, be treated as having been employed earner’s employment notwithstanding that by reason of a contravention of, or non-compliance with, some provision contained in or having effect under an enactment passed for the protection of employed persons or any class of employed persons, either—
-- src:2315 | (a) the contract purporting to govern the employment was void; or
-- src:2316 | (b) the employed person was not lawfully employed in the relevant employment at the time when, or in the place where, the accident happened or the disease or injury was contracted or received.
@ref s 97(1), s 97(2) (src:2311-2316)
GIVEN `a claim is made for industrial injuries benefit in respect of an accident, or of a prescribed disease or injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an application is made under section 29 of the Social Security Act 1998 for a declaration that an accident was an industrial accident, or for a corresponding declaration as to a prescribed disease or injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Secretary of State has directed that the relevant employment is to be treated as having been employed earner's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract purporting to govern the employment was void by reason of a contravention of, or non-compliance with, a provision of an enactment passed for the protection of employed persons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employed person was not lawfully employed in the relevant employment at the time when, or in the place where, the accident happened or the disease or injury was contracted or received, by reason of such a contravention or non-compliance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 97(2) the relevant employment is treated as employed earner's employment` MEANS
    IF   (`a claim is made for industrial injuries benefit in respect of an accident, or of a prescribed disease or injury`
          OR `an application is made under section 29 of the Social Security Act 1998 for a declaration that an accident was an industrial accident, or for a corresponding declaration as to a prescribed disease or injury`)
         AND `the Secretary of State has directed that the relevant employment is to be treated as having been employed earner's employment`
         AND (`the contract purporting to govern the employment was void by reason of a contravention of, or non-compliance with, a provision of an enactment passed for the protection of employed persons`
              OR `the employed person was not lawfully employed in the relevant employment at the time when, or in the place where, the accident happened or the disease or injury was contracted or received, by reason of such a contravention or non-compliance`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "The Secretary of State may direct"; without a claim or application, a direction, or the illegality, the section does not say what follows

§§ `s 98 -- Earner acting in breach of regulations, etc.`

-- s 98: "An accident shall be taken to arise out of and in the course of an employed earner's employment, notwithstanding that he is at the time of the accident acting in contravention of any statutory or other regulations ... or of any orders ... or ... without instructions ..., if"
-- (a) the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had the act not been done in contravention or without such instructions; and (b) the act is done for the purposes of and in connection with the employer's trade or business.
-- src:2321 | An accident shall be taken to arise out of and in the course of an employed earner’s employment, notwithstanding that he is at the time of the accident acting in contravention of any statutory or other regulations applicable to his employment, or of any orders given by or on behalf of his employer, or that he is acting without instructions from his employer, if—
-- src:2322 | (a) the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had the act not been done in contravention of any such regulations or orders, or without such instructions, as the case may be; and
-- src:2323 | (b) the act is done for the purposes of and in connection with the employer’s trade or business.
@ref s 98 (src:2321-2323)
GIVEN `the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had the act not been done in contravention of any such regulations or orders, or without such instructions, as the case may be` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the act is done for the purposes of and in connection with the employer's trade or business` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 98 the accident is taken by this section to arise out of and in the course of the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the act is done for the purposes of and in connection with the employer's trade or business`) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if ... (b) the act is done for the purposes of and in connection with the employer's trade or business"
        IF `the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had the act not been done in contravention of any such regulations or orders, or without such instructions, as the case may be` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if (a) the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had the act not been done in contravention"
        IF `the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had the act not been done in contravention of any such regulations or orders, or without such instructions, as the case may be` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the accident would have been taken so to have arisen is a judgment under section 94, not supplied

§§ `s 99 -- Earner travelling in employer's transport`

-- s 99(2): "references to a vehicle include a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft." "Include": a conveyance that is none of these is not said to be outside the word.
-- src:2330 | (2) In this section references to a vehicle include a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft.
@ref s 99(2) (src:2330)
GIVEN `the conveyance is a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 99(2) the conveyance is a vehicle for the purposes of this section` MEANS
    IF   `the conveyance is a ship, vessel, hovercraft or aircraft`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "references to a vehicle include" these; other conveyances are decided by the ordinary meaning of "vehicle", which is outside the text

-- s 99(1): "An accident happening while an employed earner is, with the express or implied permission of his employer, travelling as a passenger by any vehicle to or from his place of work shall, notwithstanding that he is under no obligation to his employer to travel by that vehicle, be taken to arise out of and in the course of his employment if"
-- (a) the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had he been under such an obligation; and (b) at the time of the accident the vehicle (i) is being operated by or on behalf of his employer or some other person by whom it is provided in pursuance of arrangements made with his employer; and (ii) is not being operated in the ordinary course of a public transport service.
-- src:2325 | (1) An accident happening while an employed earner is, with the express or implied permission of his employer, travelling as a passenger by any vehicle to or from his place of work shall, notwithstanding that he is under no obligation to his employer to travel by that vehicle, be taken to arise out of and in the course of his employment if—
-- src:2326 | (a) the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had he been under such an obligation; and
-- src:2327 | (b) at the time of the accident, the vehicle—
-- src:2328 | (i) is being operated by or on behalf of his employer or some other person by whom it is provided in pursuance of arrangements made with his employer; and
-- src:2329 | (ii) is not being operated in the ordinary course of a public transport service.
@ref s 99(1) (src:2325-2329)
GIVEN `the accident happens while the employed earner is, with the express or implied permission of his employer, travelling as a passenger to or from his place of work` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the conveyance is a vehicle for the purposes of this section` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had he been under an obligation to his employer to travel by that vehicle` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the vehicle is being operated by or on behalf of his employer or some other person by whom it is provided in pursuance of arrangements made with his employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the vehicle is being operated in the ordinary course of a public transport service` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 99(1) the accident is taken by this section to arise out of and in the course of the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the accident happens while the employed earner is, with the express or implied permission of his employer, travelling as a passenger to or from his place of work`) THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sentence is for "An accident happening while an employed earner is, with the express or implied permission of his employer, travelling as a passenger"
        IF `the conveyance is a vehicle for the purposes of this section` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sentence is for travel "by any vehicle"
        IF `the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had he been under an obligation to his employer to travel by that vehicle` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if (a) the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had he been under such an obligation"
        IF NOT (`the vehicle is being operated by or on behalf of his employer or some other person by whom it is provided in pursuance of arrangements made with his employer`) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if ... (b) at the time of the accident, the vehicle (i) is being operated by or on behalf of his employer or some other person by whom it is provided in pursuance of arrangements made with his employer"
        IF `the vehicle is being operated in the ordinary course of a public transport service` THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if ... (b) ... the vehicle ... (ii) is not being operated in the ordinary course of a public transport service"
        IF (`the conveyance is a vehicle for the purposes of this section` EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND (`the accident would have been taken so to have arisen had he been under an obligation to his employer to travel by that vehicle` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the conveyance is a vehicle, or whether the accident would have been taken so to have arisen, is not supplied

§§ `s 100 -- Accidents happening while meeting emergency`

-- s 100: "An accident happening to an employed earner in or about any premises at which he is for the time being employed for the purposes of his employer's trade or business shall be taken to arise out of and in the course of his employment if it happens while he is taking steps,
-- on an actual or supposed emergency at those premises, to rescue, succour or protect persons who are, or are thought to be or possibly to be, injured or imperilled, or to avert or minimise serious damage to property."
-- src:2332 | An accident happening to an employed earner in or about any premises at which he is for the time being employed for the purposes of his employer’s trade or business shall be taken to arise out of and in the course of his employment if it happens while he is taking steps, on an actual or supposed emergency at those premises, to rescue, succour or protect persons who are, or are thought to be or possibly to be, injured or imperilled, or to avert or minimise serious damage to property.
@ref s 100 (src:2332)
GIVEN `the accident happens to an employed earner in or about premises at which he is for the time being employed for the purposes of his employer's trade or business` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident happens while he is taking steps on an actual or supposed emergency at those premises` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the steps are to rescue, succour or protect persons who are, or are thought to be or possibly to be, injured or imperilled` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the steps are to avert or minimise serious damage to property` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 100 the accident is taken by this section to arise out of and in the course of the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the accident happens to an employed earner in or about premises at which he is for the time being employed for the purposes of his employer's trade or business`) THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sentence is for "An accident happening to an employed earner in or about any premises at which he is for the time being employed"
        IF `the accident happens while he is taking steps on an actual or supposed emergency at those premises` AND (`the steps are to rescue, succour or protect persons who are, or are thought to be or possibly to be, injured or imperilled` OR `the steps are to avert or minimise serious damage to property`) THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if it happens while he is taking steps, on an actual or supposed emergency at those premises, to rescue, succour or protect persons ... or to avert or minimise serious damage to property"

§§ `s 101 -- Accident caused by another's misconduct etc.`

-- s 101: "An accident happening after 19th December 1961 shall be treated for the purposes of industrial injuries benefit, where it would not apart from this section be so treated, as arising out of an employed earner's employment if"
-- (a) the accident arises in the course of the employment; and (b) the accident either is caused (i) by another person's misconduct, skylarking or negligence, or (ii) by steps taken in consequence of any such misconduct, skylarking or negligence, or (iii) by the behaviour or presence of an animal (including a bird, fish or insect), or is caused by or consists in the employed earner being struck by any object or by lightning; and
-- (c) the employed earner did not directly or indirectly induce or contribute to the happening of the accident by his conduct outside the employment or by any act not incidental to the employment.
-- src:2334 | An accident happening after 19th December 1961 shall be treated for the purposes of industrial injuries benefit, where it would not apart from this section be so treated, as arising out of an employed earner’s employment if—
-- src:2335 | (a) the accident arises in the course of the employment; and
-- src:2336 | (b) the accident either is caused—
-- src:2337 | (i) by another person’s misconduct, skylarking or negligence, or
-- src:2338 | (ii) by steps taken in consequence of any such misconduct, skylarking or negligence, or
-- src:2339 | (iii) by the behaviour or presence of an animal (including a bird, fish or insect),
-- src:2340 | or is caused by or consists in the employed earner being struck by any object or by lightning; and
-- src:2341 | (c) the employed earner did not directly or indirectly induce or contribute to the happening of the accident by his conduct outside the employment or by any act not incidental to the employment.
@ref s 101 (src:2334-2341)
GIVEN `the date of the accident` IS A DATE
      `apart from this section the accident would be treated as arising out of an employed earner's employment` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the accident arises in the course of the employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident is caused by another person's misconduct, skylarking or negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident is caused by steps taken in consequence of any such misconduct, skylarking or negligence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident is caused by the behaviour or presence of an animal (including a bird, fish or insect)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident is caused by or consists in the employed earner being struck by any object or by lightning` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employed earner directly or indirectly induced or contributed to the happening of the accident by his conduct outside the employment or by any act not incidental to the employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 101 the accident is treated by this section as arising out of the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the date of the accident` GREATER THAN Date 19 12 1961) THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the section is for "An accident happening after 19th December 1961"
        IF `apart from this section the accident would be treated as arising out of an employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the section is for the case "where it would not apart from this section be so treated"
        IF NOT (`the accident arises in the course of the employment`) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if (a) the accident arises in the course of the employment"
        IF NOT (`the accident is caused by another person's misconduct, skylarking or negligence` OR `the accident is caused by steps taken in consequence of any such misconduct, skylarking or negligence` OR `the accident is caused by the behaviour or presence of an animal (including a bird, fish or insect)` OR `the accident is caused by or consists in the employed earner being struck by any object or by lightning`) THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if ... (b) the accident either is caused ... or is caused by or consists in the employed earner being struck by any object or by lightning"
        IF `the employed earner directly or indirectly induced or contributed to the happening of the accident by his conduct outside the employment or by any act not incidental to the employment` THEN JUST FALSE    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if ... (c) the employed earner did not directly or indirectly induce or contribute to the happening of the accident"
        IF `apart from this section the accident would be treated as arising out of an employed earner's employment` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the accident would be so treated apart from the section is a judgment under section 94, not supplied
